    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the classic, historical surgical procedure described by Fujita in 1981 for the treatment of retropalatal obstruction in OSA?', '["Tracheostomy", "Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP)", "Maxillomandibular advancement", "Midline glossectomy", "Nasal septoplasty"]'::jsonb, 1, 'UPPP was the first widely performed sleep surgery. It involves excising the tonsils, trimming the free margin of the soft palate and uvula, and suturing the tonsillar pillars together to widen the retropalatal space.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a major, tissue-preserving modification of the classic UPPP, designed to reduce post-operative pain and avoid scarring/velopharyngeal insufficiency?', '["Total glossectomy", "The Uvulopalatal Flap (UPF) or Anterior Palatoplasty", "Bilateral neck dissection", "Cricothyroidotomy", "Laryngeal suspension"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Modern sleep surgery has shifted away from aggressive tissue excision (classic UPPP). Procedures like the Uvulopalatal Flap (UPF) peel back the mucosa and fold the uvula/palate forward, stenting the airway open while preserving the underlying muscle and vascularity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient who underwent a classic UPPP 2 weeks ago complains that every time they drink water, it shoots out of their nose. What is this complication called?', '["Dysphagia", "Velopharyngeal Insufficiency (VPI)", "Nasopharyngeal stenosis", "Aspiration pneumonia", "Esophageal spasm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'VPI occurs when too much of the soft palate is removed or scarred, preventing the palate from fully sealing against the posterior pharyngeal wall during swallowing. While often transient, aggressive palatal resection can cause permanent VPI.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a rare, highly challenging long-term complication of over-aggressive palatal surgery (classic UPPP), resulting in severe nasal obstruction, hyponasal speech, and difficulty swallowing?', '["Velopharyngeal insufficiency", "Nasopharyngeal Stenosis (circumferential scarring of the nasopharynx to the palate)", "Vocal cord paralysis", "Laryngomalacia", "Tongue atrophy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nasopharyngeal stenosis is a devastating complication where the raw, mucosalized edges of the palate and posterior pharyngeal wall scar together, closing off the nasopharynx. It is highly difficult to reconstruct.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary target muscle manipulated during an "Expansion Sphincter Pharyngoplasty" (ESP), and how is it relocated to expand the lateral pharyngeal walls?', '["The genioglossus muscle; pulled forward", "The Palatopharyngeus muscle; it is transected, rotated superiorly and laterally, and sutured to the pterygoid hamulus to pull the lateral pharyngeal walls outward", "The superior constrictor; removed entirely", "The cricothyroid; tensed", "The tensor veli palatini; cut"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ESP is a highly effective lateral pharyngoplasty technique. By mobilizing the palatopharyngeus muscle (which forms the posterior tonsillar pillar) and anchoring it forward/laterally, the lateral walls of the throat are physically pulled open, preventing lateral collapse.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'For which group of patients are palatal stiffening procedures (such as Pillar implants or radiofrequency ablation of the soft palate) most appropriate?', '["Morbidly obese patients with severe OSA", "Patients with primary snoring or very mild OSA who have isolated palatal flutter", "Patients with severe retrolingual collapse", "Children with Down syndrome", "Patients with central sleep apnea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pillar implants (inserting tiny dacron rods into the soft palate) and radiofrequency ablation aim to scar and stiffen the palate, reducing snoring vibrations. They are ineffective for moderate-to-severe OSA or multi-level obstruction.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of isolated nasal surgery (e.g., septoplasty and bilateral inferior turbinate reduction) in an obese patient with severe OSA?', '["It is a highly reliable cure that will eliminate their sleep apnea", "It rarely cures the OSA on its own, but significantly reduces nasal airway resistance, makes nasal breathing easier, and drastically improves the patient''s ability to tolerate and comply with CPAP therapy", "It converts OSA into central sleep apnea", "It causes weight loss", "It paralyzes the soft palate to stop snoring"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a crucial patient counseling point. Nasal surgery is a "gateway" surgery for OSA. It lowers the CPAP pressure requirements and stops mouth breathing, but the patient must understand they will still need their CPAP post-operatively.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the number one, most common etiology of Obstructive Sleep Apnea in pediatric patients (ages 2 to 8 years)?', '["Obesity", "Adenotonsillar Hypertrophy (enlarged tonsils and adenoids)", "Micrognathia", "Hypothyroidism", "Neuromuscular weakness"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Unlike adults, where obesity and tissue laxity dominate, pediatric OSA is overwhelmingly driven by lymphoid tissue hypertrophy (tonsils and adenoids) crowding the relatively small pediatric pharynx.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the first-line, standard of care treatment for a child diagnosed with symptomatic Obstructive Sleep Apnea secondary to adenotonsillar hypertrophy?', '["CPAP therapy", "Adenotonsillectomy (T&A)", "Rapid maxillary expansion", "Tracheostomy", "Intranasal steroids alone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Surgical removal of the tonsils and adenoids is highly successful in children, curing or dramatically improving OSA in over 80-90% of healthy pediatric patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'According to pediatric sleep guidelines, which of the following children is at a highly elevated risk for post-operative respiratory complications following an adenotonsillectomy, mandating an overnight hospital admission rather than outpatient surgery?', '["A healthy 6-year-old with mild OSA", "An infant under age 3, or a child with severe OSA (AHI > 10, nadir SpO2 < 80 percent), obesity, craniofacial anomalies, or neuromuscular disorders", "A child who lives close to the hospital", "A child with a normal BMI", "A child who has never had an ear infection"]'::jsonb, 1, 'High-risk pediatric patients are prone to post-operative airway obstruction, pulmonary edema, and severe oxygen desaturations. They must be monitored overnight in a pediatric ward or ICU.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the physiologic cause of "Post-Obstructive Pulmonary Edema" (POPE Type I) that can occur immediately after a tonsillectomy or tracheostomy in a patient with severe OSA?', '["A viral infection", "The sudden relief of chronic upper airway obstruction; the rapid drop in intrathoracic pressure and changes in pulmonary blood volume cause fluid to shift out of the capillaries and flood the alveoli", "Fluid overload from IV fluids only", "Allergic reaction to anesthesia", "Heart failure from blood loss"]'::jsonb, 1, 'POPE (also known as negative-pressure pulmonary edema) is a feared complication. Chronic struggling against a closed airway keeps intrathoracic pressures highly negative. When the airway is suddenly opened, the pressure shifts cause rapid alveolar flooding. It is treated with positive-pressure ventilation (CPAP/intubation) and oxygen.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does the scoring of an "apnea" in children differ from adults on a polysomnography report?', '["In children, the pause must last at least 20 seconds", "In children, the pause in breathing only needs to last for the duration of 2 missed breaths (typically 4-6 seconds) to be scored as an apnea", "Children do not have central apneas", "Children require a 5% drop in oxygen to score an apnea", "There is no difference"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because children breathe faster and have a much lower functional residual capacity (oxygen reservoir), even a very brief pause in breathing causes rapid hypoxia and is pathologically significant.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which dental procedure, performed by an orthodontist, is highly effective for treating pediatric OSA in children with a narrow, high-arched hard palate and a dental crossbite?', '["Tooth extraction", "Rapid Maxillary Expansion (RME)", "Wisdom teeth removal", "A simple mouthguard", "Root canal therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'RME uses an adjustable appliance anchored to the molars to slowly widen the suture of the hard palate. This physically expands the nasal cavity floor and the oral cavity, reducing nasal resistance and leaving more room for the tongue.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In children with severe, refractory OSA who have failed adenotonsillectomy (T&A) and cannot tolerate CPAP, what is the most common site of persistent obstruction visualized on DISE?', '["The nasal valve", "The tongue base, lingual tonsils, or supraglottic tissues (dynamic collapse)", "The vocal cords", "The trachea", "The lower esophagus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Persistent pediatric OSA after T&A is common, especially in obese children or those with syndromic conditions. DISE is used to identify secondary sites, which are often lingual tonsil hypertrophy or floppy supraglottic tissues.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Coblator-assisted tonsillectomy" (Coblation)?', '["A method of freezing the tonsils", "A surgical technique that uses radiofrequency energy in a saline medium to gently dissolve (ablate) tonsil tissue at a low temperature (40-70 degrees C), reducing surrounding thermal damage compared to standard electrocautery", "The use of a cold steel scalpel only", "The use of a laser", "The use of a drill"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Coblation operates at much lower temperatures than standard Monopolar Electrocautery (which burns tissue at 400 degrees C). This minimizes collateral tissue damage and can significantly reduce post-operative pain and speed recovery.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which cranial nerve is at risk of temporary or permanent injury during a deep lateral pharyngoplasty, potentially causing tongue deviation or swallowing difficulties?', '["The Facial nerve (CN VII)", "The Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) or Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)", "The Vagus nerve (CN X) only", "The Accessory nerve (CN XI)", "The Trigeminal nerve (CN V)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The glossopharyngeal nerve runs in the lateral pharyngeal wall, and the hypoglossal nerve lies close to the deep submandibular space. Careful dissection is required to protect these structures during aggressive muscle transposition.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 5-year-old child presents with snoring, mouth breathing, and a chronic hyponasal "stuffy nose" voice. When they speak, they sound like they have a cold, even when healthy. What tissue hypertrophy is most likely?', '["Lingual tonsil hypertrophy", "Adenoid Hypertrophy", "Vocal cord nodules", "Septal deviation", "Turbinate hypertrophy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The adenoids sit in the nasopharynx. Hypertrophy blocks the back of the nose, forcing mouth breathing and eliminating normal nasal resonance (causing hyponasal speech).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of intranasal corticosteroids (e.g., Fluticasone) and oral leukotriene receptor antagonists (e.g., Montelukast) in pediatric OSA?', '["They are the primary cure for severe OSA", "They can be used as a first-line medical therapy for mild pediatric OSA, helping to shrink adenoid/tonsil lymphoid tissue and reduce airway inflammation, potentially avoiding surgery", "They are strictly contraindicated in children", "They cause sleepwalking", "They have no effect on lymphoid tissue"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For children with very mild OSA (AHI < 2-3) or those where surgery is delayed, a trial of nasal steroids and montelukast can successfully reduce lymphoid swelling and resolve the sleep apnea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is a history of Down Syndrome (Trisomy 21) critical when planning a pediatric adenotonsillectomy?', '["Down syndrome children do not have adenoids", "These children have a very high rate of persistent OSA after surgery due to macroglossia and hypotonia, and they have atlantoaxial instability, requiring careful neck positioning during intubation", "They are immune to post-op bleeding", "They require higher doses of local anesthetics", "They always require a tracheostomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Down syndrome patients require extreme care. Standard hyper-extension of the neck during tonsillectomy can cause catastrophic spinal cord injury if they have undiagnosed atlantoaxial joint laxity. Furthermore, T&A cures their OSA in only about 30-50% of cases, requiring long-term follow-up.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the standard, post-operative "danger window" for delayed primary or secondary hemorrhage following a tonsillectomy?', '["Only the first 2 hours", "Between post-operative days 5 and 10, when the surgical fibrin eschar (scab) sloughs off the raw muscle bed", "Exactly 24 hours later", "After 1 month", "There is no risk of bleeding after the first day"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Secondary tonsillar hemorrhage is a classic, high-yield ENT emergency. As the scab dissolves around day 7, exposed vessels can bleed. Patients and parents must be warned to present immediately to the ED if any active bleeding occurs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 4-year-old child who underwent a tonsillectomy 7 days ago is brought to the ED spitting up bright red blood. What is the most appropriate initial management for a minor, self-limiting post-tonsillectomy bleed?', '["Immediate return to the operating room", "Observation, keeping the patient NPO, checking coagulation labs, and applying topically applied vasoconstrictors (like oxymetazoline or thrombin) if a visible clot is present; prepare for OR if bleeding is active or severe", "Give a warm cup of tea", "Send the patient home with antibiotics", "Perform a tracheostomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Minor oozing or a small clot can often be managed conservatively with observation and cold gargles, but the clinician must be highly vigilant as a minor bleed can be a "sentinel bleed" before a massive, life-threatening hemorrhage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In adult patients with severe OSA, what is the reported "success rate" of a standard UPPP alone (defined as a ≥50 percent reduction in AHI and a final AHI < 20)?', '["Over 90 percent", "Approximately 40 to 50 percent overall, highlighting the need for careful patient selection (e.g., Friedman Stage I)", "Less than 5 percent", "100 percent in all patients", "UPPP has been completely abandoned and is never performed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Without candidate selection, unselected UPPP is essentially a coin flip. This poor historic success rate is what drove the development of staging systems, lateral pharyngoplasty, and multi-level surgery.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA undergoes UPPP. At their 3-month post-op PSG, their AHI has dropped from 60 to 45. While they report snoring less, does this represent a surgical "cure"?', '["Yes, because the AHI dropped", "No, this is a surgical failure; a AHI of 45 still represents severe OSA with high cardiovascular risk, and they must resume CPAP or seek alternative therapies", "Yes, because the snoring stopped", "Yes, they can stop all treatments", "No, they need an immediate tracheostomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Snoring is a poor indicator of success. The patient''s partner may be happy, but the patient''s heart and brain are still suffering severe sleep apnea. They must understand that "snoring less" does not equal "cured."'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following describes the "Fujita Classification" of upper airway obstruction?', '["Type I: glottic; Type II: subglottic; Type III: tracheal", "Type I: palatal only (velopharyngeal); Type II: multi-level (palatal and tongue base); Type III: tongue base only (hypopharyngeal)", "Type I: mild; Type II: moderate; Type III: severe", "Type I: nasal; Type II: pharyngeal; Type III: laryngeal", "Type I: pediatric; Type II: adult; Type III: geriatric"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Fujita classification categorizes the site of collapse: Type I (palate), Type II (both palate and tongue base), and Type III (tongue base). Standard UPPP only treats Type I. Most failed UPPPs were actually Type II patients whose tongue base obstruction was ignored.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary anatomical structure removed during an "adenoidectomy"?', '["The palatine tonsils", "The pharyngeal tonsil (lymphoid tissue located on the roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx)", "The lingual tonsils", "The uvula", "The thyroid gland"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The adenoids (pharyngeal tonsil) sit in the back of the nasal cavity. Adenoidectomy is performed using a curette, suction coagulator, or coblator to clear the nasopharyngeal airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which artery is the primary source of arterial blood supply to the palatine tonsils, and is the most common source of severe post-tonsillectomy arterial bleeding?', '["The superior thyroid artery", "The tonsillar branch of the facial artery", "The ascending pharyngeal artery", "The internal carotid artery", "The lingual artery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tonsillar branch of the facial artery enters the lower pole of the tonsil. It is a high-pressure artery, and failure to secure it during surgery is a primary cause of major intraoperative or post-operative hemorrhage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA is found to have "Retro-palatal lateral wall collapse" on DISE. What modern pharyngoplasty technique is specifically designed to address this by reinforcing the lateral walls with a suture suspension?', '["Classic UPPP", "Zeta-Pharyngoplasty or Lateral Pharyngoplasty", "Tracheostomy", "Midline glossectomy", "Nasal septoplasty"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Lateral pharyngoplasty (such as Zeta-pharyngoplasty or suture-suspension techniques) physically anchors the lateral pharyngeal tissues to the pterygomandibular raphe, preventing the side walls of the throat from falling inward.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Anterior Palatoplasty" (also known as the suture re-tensioning technique)?', '["The removal of the entire soft palate", "A surgical procedure where a strip of mucosa is removed from the oral side of the soft palate, and the remaining tissue is sutured to pull the palate forward and upward, stiffening it", "A skeletal surgery of the jaw", "A nasal surgery", "The injection of Botox into the palate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Anterior palatoplasty is a highly effective, low-morbidity palatal stiffening and shortening technique. It preserves the muscles of the palate while physically pulling it out of the airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a UPPP, why must the surgeon be extremely careful not to injure the "Levator Veli Palatini" muscle?', '["Because it causes tongue paralysis", "Injury to this muscle can cause permanent velopharyngeal insufficiency (VPI) or severe scarring, as this muscle is crucial for elevating the palate during swallowing and speech", "Because it supplies blood to the brain", "Because it is required for breathing", "Because it causes vocal cord paralysis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The levator muscle must be preserved. Classic UPPP often damaged this muscle, leading to the high rates of chronic swallowing and speech deficits seen in the 1980s and 1990s. Modern techniques focus on mucosal trimming and muscular preservation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A child with a history of cleft palate repair is diagnosed with severe OSA. What palatal surgical procedure is generally considered a relative CONTRAINDICATION in this patient?', '["Adenotonsillectomy", "Standard UPPP (or any aggressive palatal shortening), due to an extremely high risk of inducing permanent, severe velopharyngeal insufficiency (VPI) and hypernasal speech", "Rapid maxillary expansion", "Tracheostomy", "Nasal turbinate reduction"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In cleft palate patients, the palate is already structurally short or dysfunctional. Shortening it further with UPPP will guarantee they cannot seal their nasopharynx, causing severe, permanent hypernasal speech and nasal regurgitation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A child undergoes a tonsillectomy. On post-op day 1, they are refusing to drink any liquids due to severe pain. What is the most critical risk for this patient?', '["Immediate weight gain", "Dehydration and a resulting increased risk of secondary hemorrhage (as dry throat tissues are prone to cracking and bleeding)", "Vocal cord paralysis", "Adenoid regrowth", "Permanent loss of voice"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pain control is paramount after pediatric tonsillectomy. If the child refuses to drink, they dehydrate. Dehydration dries out the oral scabs, causing them to slough off prematurely and trigger massive bleeding. Keeping them hydrated with cold, sweet liquids is the most important post-op goal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of "Intracapsular (Partial) Tonsillectomy" (tonsillotomy) compared to standard extracapsular (total) tonsillectomy in children?', '["It is only performed in adults", "It involves shaving away 90-95 percent of the tonsil tissue using a microdebrider or coblator, leaving the tonsil capsule intact. This significantly reduces post-op pain and bleeding risk while maintaining equal effectiveness for pediatric OSA", "It has a much higher rate of cancer", "It requires a larger skin incision", "It is an in-office awake procedure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Partial tonsillectomy has become highly popular. Because the underlying muscle bed and large vessels are not exposed, the child has significantly less pain, returns to a normal diet faster, and has a near-zero rate of delayed hemorrhage. The small risk is tonsil regrowth (<3-5%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why are children with severe OSA at risk for developing "cor pulmonale" (right-sided heart failure) if left untreated?', '["Children have weak heart valves", "Chronic nighttime hypoxia causes persistent pulmonary vasoconstriction, leading to pulmonary hypertension, right ventricular hypertrophy, and eventually right-sided heart failure", "OSA causes direct viral myocarditis", "OSA stops blood flow to the kidneys", "Children do not get cor pulmonale"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cor pulmonale is a classic, severe manifestation of neglected pediatric OSA. Fortunately, it is highly reversible once the airway is opened (T&A).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old obese male with severe OSA undergoes a tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy. His post-op PSG 3 months later shows his AHI has dropped from 40 to 18. Why is his AHI still elevated?', '["The tonsils grew back in 3 months", "Obese children frequently have persistent OSA after T&A due to generalized pharyngeal crowding and retroglossic collapse, requiring weight loss, CPAP, or secondary surgeries", "The surgery was performed incorrectly", "He has central sleep apnea only", "The sleep study is an artifact"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While T&A is highly successful in thin children (~90% cure), it cures only about 30-50% of obese children. Their underlying airway narrowness and fat tissue remain, requiring continued therapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which muscle forms the anterior tonsillar pillar, and is often preserved or re-approximated during pharyngoplasty?', '["Palatopharyngeus", "Palatoglossus", "Stylopharyngeus", "Superior constrictor", "Genioglossus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The palatoglossus muscle forms the anterior pillar. The palatopharyngeus forms the posterior pillar. These muscles are the anatomical boundaries of the tonsillar fossa.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Nasopharyngeal Stenosis" following UPPP?', '["A narrowing of the nasal cavity", "An acquired, severe circumferential scarring and narrowing of the connection between the oropharynx and nasopharynx, caused by combining UPPP with aggressive adenoidectomy or removing too much mucosal tissue", "A type of sinus infection", "A complication of nasal sprays", "A thyroid condition"]'::jsonb, 1, 'NP stenosis is a nightmare complication. The raw edges of the palate and posterior throat wall heal together, blocking the nasal airway. It requires complex surgical reconstruction, often with local flaps (like a pharyngeal flap or palatal rotation flaps) to line the scarred area with healthy mucosa.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is considered a safe, first-line analgesic choice for post-operative pain control in a child after a tonsillectomy, according to modern FDA safety warnings?', '["Codeine", "Acetaminophen and Ibuprofen (scheduled alternate dosing)", "High-dose oral morphine only", "Aspirin", "Tramadol"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The FDA has placed a black box warning on codeine for pediatric post-tonsillectomy pain. Some children are "ultra-rapid metabolizers" who convert codeine to morphine extremely fast in their liver, leading to fatal respiratory depression during sleep. Scheduled acetaminophen and ibuprofen are highly effective and safe.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient who underwent a UPPP 1 week ago presents to the clinic complaining that "everything I eat tastes completely different or like metal." What is the most likely cause?', '["The patient has developed tongue cancer", "Transient dysgeusia (taste disturbance) is a common side effect of UPPP, typically caused by pressure on the glossopharyngeal nerve or tongue during tongue retraction in surgery, and usually resolves within 3-6 months", "The CPAP machine is blowing metal dust", "The patient is dehydrated", "The patient has a fungal infection"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Taste disturbance is a common, yet rarely discussed, side effect of palatal and tonsil surgery. It is almost always self-limiting as the retracted nerves recover from stretch injury.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Anterior Palatoplasty" (the Roman Borders technique)?', '["A surgery to remove the teeth", "A palatal reshaping technique that uses lateral mucosal incisions and suture anchoring to pull the soft palate forward and widen the retropalatal space without removing the uvula", "A bony surgery of the maxilla", "A nasal valve surgery", "A type of tracheostomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Like the uvulopalatal flap, the Roman Borders anterior palatoplasty focuses on pulling the palate forward out of the airway while preserving the uvula, maintaining normal speech and swallowing function.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which surgical instrument uses radiofrequency energy to cut and coagulate tissue at the same time, and is widely used for tonsillectomy due to its precise control and low thermal spread?', '["Monopolar electrocautery", "Harmonic Scalpel (ultrasonic) or Coblation", "A cold steel scalpel", "A carbon dioxide laser", "A bone drill"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Harmonic scalpel (which vibrates ultrasonically to cut and seal vessels) and Coblation are highly favored modern instruments that minimize deep tissue burns, helping to reduce the severe post-operative pain associated with standard electrocautery.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is a history of craniofacial syndromes (such as Treacher Collins or Apert syndrome) critical when evaluating a child for sleep apnea surgery?', '["These children do not have sleep apnea", "They have severe skeletal midface/mandibular hypoplasia, meaning a standard T&A is highly unlikely to fully cure their OSA. They almost always require skeletal surgery or long-term CPAP", "They are immune to anesthesia", "They do not have tonsils", "They always require immediate tracheostomy at birth"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In syndromic children, the primary issue is bony airway crowding. While a T&A is performed to remove any lymphoid obstruction, the surgeon must set realistic expectations that further skeletal advancement (like distraction osteogenesis) will likely be needed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary anatomical boundary that separates the "oropharynx" from the "nasopharynx"?', '["The hard palate", "The free margin of the soft palate and the uvula", "The base of the tongue", "The epiglottis", "The hyoid bone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The soft palate acts as a swing-door. When elevated, it seals against the posterior throat wall, separating the nose cavity (nasopharynx) from the mouth cavity (oropharynx).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with severe OSA presents for follow-up 6 months after UPPP. Their partner reports they still snore softly, but they no longer gasp or stop breathing. What is the most appropriate next step?', '["Assume they are cured and discharge them", "Order a repeat polysomnography (PSG) to objectively assess their residual AHI and oxygen levels, as snoring volume does not correlate with OSA resolution", "Perform a tracheostomy", "Switch them to an oral appliance without a study", "Increase their CPAP pressure blindly"]'::jsonb, 1, 'You cannot diagnose "success" based on subjective snoring. A post-operative sleep study is mandatory for all moderate-to-severe OSA patients to prove the cardiovascular risk has been eliminated.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is "Post-operative airway obstruction" in the PACU following sleep surgery?', '["The patient has forgotten how to breathe", "The sudden, life-threatening collapse of the swollen upper airway tissues immediately after extubation, exacerbated by residual anesthetic agents and narcotics", "A normal finding that should be ignored", "A side effect of antibiotics", "A symptom of a panic attack"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is the most feared immediate complication of sleep surgery. Because the airway has just been operated on, it is swollen and bleeding. Sedating medications suppress the patient''s already compromised drive to keep their throat open, leading to rapid asphyxiation. The surgical team must be immediately available with airway equipment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following pediatric patients with OSA is a candidate for "sleep study-free" adenotonsillectomy?', '["An obese 10-year-old", "A child with Down syndrome", "A healthy child with clear physical evidence of adenotonsillar hypertrophy and a history of classic witnessed apneas/snoring", "A child with a normal throat exam", "A child with severe asthma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For a completely healthy child with obvious large tonsils and classic symptoms, a pre-operative sleep study is not strictly mandatory, and the surgeon can proceed straight to T&A. However, if there is any diagnostic doubt or comorbid condition, a PSG is required.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "surgical cure rate" (defined as AHI < 5) of adenotonsillectomy in healthy, non-obese children with OSA?', '["Less than 20 percent", "Approximately 80 to 90 percent, representing the most successful soft tissue surgery in sleep medicine", "Exactly 50 percent", "100 percent in all children", "0 percent; children always require CPAP"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In healthy, thin children, T&A is highly curative. This is because removing the large lymphoid block completely restores a normal, spacious pediatric airway.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which major vessel runs deep in the lateral pharyngeal space, approximately 2-3 cm lateral to the tonsillar fossa, and is at risk during aggressive lateral pharyngoplasty?', '["The external jugular vein", "The Internal Carotid Artery (ICA)", "The superior thyroid artery", "The subclavian artery", "The vertebral artery"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ICA lies in the postero-lateral neck space. Aggressive, deep, or blind suture placement in the lateral pharyngeal wall can puncture this massive artery, causing catastrophic, fatal hemorrhage.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A parent brings in a 4-year-old child who is a restless sleeper, sweats heavily at night, and sleeps in strange positions (e.g., neck hyperextended). Their AHI is 3. What is the diagnosis and recommended management?', '["Mild pediatric OSA; treat with nasal steroids/montelukast or trial of adenotonsillectomy if symptomatic", "Normal childhood behavior; reassure the parents", "Severe central sleep apnea; require mechanical ventilation", "ADHD; start stimulants", "Narcolepsy; start modafinil"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Restless sleep and nighttime sweating are common, high-yield symptoms of pediatric OSA. Children will often hyperextend their neck to physically pull the jaw and epiglottis off their airway. An AHI of 3 is abnormal in a child and warrants treatment if symptomatic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why is standard "cautery loops" (total removal of tonsil tissue down to the muscle) associated with more post-operative pain than partial "intracapsular" tonsillectomy?', '["Cautery loops expose the raw constrictor muscle bed and glossopharyngeal nerve endings to the air and acidic saliva, triggering severe muscle spasms and intense pain", "Partial tonsillectomy uses a larger incision", "Cautery loops use cold steel", "Partial tonsillectomy burns more tissue", "Cautery loops cause more infections"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Exposing the raw muscle bed of the pharyngeal constrictors leads to intense spasm every time the child swallows. Intracapsular tonsillectomy leaves a natural "band-aid" of tonsil capsule over the muscle, dramatically reducing pain and allowing the child to eat normally.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient presents to the clinic 1 year after UPPP. They complain of a constant "globus sensation" (feeling like something is stuck in their throat) and persistent mild dysphagia. What is the likely cause?', '["Regrowth of the tonsils", "Chronic tissue scarring, tightness, or altered pharyngeal sensation/reflexes resulting from the surgical resection of the soft palate and uvula", "The CPAP pressure is too high", "A new throat tumor", "Reflux only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Globus sensation and swallowing tightness are recognized long-term sequelae of classic UPPP. The removal of the uvula and scarring of the palate alters the delicate sensory and motor loops of the pharynx. This is why modern surgeons avoid aggressive tissue removal.');
